var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"physics/overview/#physics-overview","page":"Model Overview","text":"QGYBJ+.jl couples quasigeostrophic balanced flow to a phase-averaged near-inertial-wave envelope.","title":"Physics overview"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/overview/#State-variables","page":"Model Overview","text":"Field Space Meaning\nq spectral prognostic generalized potential vorticity\nB spectral prognostic complex wave envelope\npsi spectral streamfunction diagnosed from balanced PV\nA, C spectral diagnosed wave amplitude and vertical derivative\nu, v, w physical diagnosed velocity components\n\nWith two-way feedback, q contains balanced and wave contributions:\n\nq = q^g + q^w\nqquad\nq^g = nabla_h^2psi\n    + partial_zleft(fracf_0^2N^2partial_zpsiright)\n\nThe total PV evolves as\n\npartial_t q + J(psiq) = mathcal D_q\n\nThe wave equation is\n\npartial_t B + J(psiB)\n= -fraci2zeta B\n  -fraci f_02nabla_h^2 A\n  + mathcal D_B\nqquad zeta=nabla_h^2psi\n\nSee QG equations, YBJ⁺ wave model, and wave–mean interaction for definitions and coupling details.","title":"State variables"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/overview/#Configurable-dynamics","page":"Model Overview","text":"FixedFlow() or EvolvingFlow() controls balanced-flow evolution.\nYBJPlus(), YBJ(), or PassiveWave() selects wave dynamics.\nWaveMeanFeedback() includes wave PV in balanced inversion; NoWaveFeedback() omits it.\nNonlinearDynamics() or LinearDynamics() controls nonlinear advection.\nDispersive() or NoDispersion() controls wave dispersion.\nDissipative() or Inviscid() controls configured closures.","title":"Configurable dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/overview/#Domain-and-boundary-conditions","page":"Model Overview","text":"Horizontal directions are periodic. Vertical nodes are cell centered between a rigid bottom and surface, with z=0 at the surface. The balanced streamfunction uses homogeneous Neumann conditions at top and bottom; vertical velocity vanishes at those boundaries.","title":"Domain and boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#io-output","page":"I/O and Output","text":"","title":"I/O and restart"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#Scheduled-NetCDF-snapshots","page":"I/O and Output","text":"output = NetCDFOutput(\n    path=\"output\",\n    schedule=IterationInterval(100),\n    fields=(:ψ, :waves),\n    velocities=true,\n)\nsimulation = Simulation(model; Δt=10.0, stop_iteration=1000,\n                        output=output)\n\nTimeInterval(seconds) provides time-based scheduling instead. The initial condition is written when output is enabled, and finalization writes the last iteration if it was not already scheduled.\n\nTime schedules keep a nominal next deadline. If a step crosses a deadline, one snapshot is written at that step and the next deadline advances from the schedule rather than from the late write, avoiding cumulative drift.\n\nA run never silently replaces files already in its output directory. A simulation restored from a state file (see Restart) continues the numbering after the files up to its restart iteration. Any other existing state files raise an error, unless the output is configured with NetCDFOutput(...; overwrite=true), which removes them first: all of them for a run that starts at iteration 0, and only those after the restart iteration for a restored run.","title":"Scheduled NetCDF snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#Energy-diagnostics","page":"I/O and Output","text":"Passing a schedule as diagnostics records initial, scheduled, and final energy values in diagnostic/ beneath the Eulerian output directory:\n\nsimulation = Simulation(\n    model;\n    output=NetCDFOutput(path=\"output\"),\n    diagnostics=IterationInterval(10),\n)\n\nThe directory contains wave_KE.nc, wave_PE.nc, wave_CE.nc, mean_flow_KE.nc, mean_flow_PE.nc, and total_energy.nc. Configure an independent location with EnergyDiagnosticsOutput(path=\"energies\", schedule=TimeInterval(600.0)). The files are rewritten during the run, at most every ten seconds of wall time, and once more when it ends, so a run that is stopped early keeps its series. A restored simulation appends to the series already there; other existing series need overwrite=true (which a schedule passed as diagnostics takes from the NetCDFOutput). The summary file preserves the component series together with total_wave_energy, total_flow_energy, coupled_energy, and total_energy. coupled_energy = total_flow_energy + wave_PE + wave_CE is the wave–mean-feedback conservation diagnostic of Asselin & Young (2019), equation (3.7), for the ideal inviscid coupled system; it excludes the informational wave_KE series. The existing total_energy remains the sum of every reported flow and wave component.","title":"Energy diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#Snapshot-schema","page":"I/O and Output","text":"Every file contains x, y, z, z_face, time, iteration metadata, spectral q_hat_real, q_hat_imag, B_hat_real, and B_hat_imag, plus N2, N2_face, and a_ell. The spectral fields are the unnormalised horizontal FFTs of q and B, in FFTW order, on the dimensions (kx_index, ky_index, z); the variables kx and ky hold the wavenumbers. Every variable has a long_name. The model has no fixed units, so the files carry none: the units_convention attribute records that every quantity is in the units of the model configuration. N2 is sampled at cell centers on z; N2_face and a_ell = f0^2 / N2_face are sampled at each cell's upper face on z_face = grid.z_faces[2:end]. The Lx, Ly, and Lz attributes record the domain extent. feedback_mode records the configured feedback component, while generalized_pv distinguishes balanced-only PV from total PV containing the wave contribution.\n\nSelecting :ψ adds physical psi. Selecting :waves adds physical A_real, A_imag, LA_real, and LA_imag, where L A = partial_z(f_0^2N^2)partial_z A. velocities=true adds u, v, and w, all at cell centres on z; w is the QG omega-equation velocity, solved on the faces and averaged onto the centres.\n\nArray dimensions in NetCDF are (x, y, z); model arrays use (z, x, y). The wave_formulation attribute identifies the meaning of B: for YBJPlus, B=L^+A; for YBJ, B=LA.","title":"Snapshot schema"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#Offline-Fourier-analysis","page":"I/O and Output","text":"Each file stores the wavenumbers of its Fourier coefficients, in FFTW order:\n\nusing NCDatasets\nusing FFTW\n\nNCDataset(\"output/state0001.nc\", \"r\") do ds\n    kx, ky = ds[\"kx\"][:], ds[\"ky\"][:]\n    A = ds[\"A_real\"][:, :, :] .+ im .* ds[\"A_imag\"][:, :, :]\n    LA = ds[\"LA_real\"][:, :, :] .+ im .* ds[\"LA_imag\"][:, :, :]\n\n    forward = FFTW.plan_fft(A, (1, 2))\n    backward = FFTW.plan_ifft(A, (1, 2))\n    A_hat = forward * A\n    LA_hat = forward * LA\n    kh² = reshape(kx .^ 2, :, 1, 1) .+\n          reshape(ky .^ 2, 1, :, 1)\n    LplusA_hat = LA_hat .- 0.25 .* kh² .* A_hat\n    LplusA = backward * LplusA_hat\nend\n\nFor a YBJ⁺ snapshot, LplusA_hat agrees with B_hat_real + im * B_hat_imag. The FFT dimensions are (1, 2) because NetCDF fields use (x, y, z) order.","title":"Offline Fourier analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#io-restart","page":"I/O and Output","text":"Build a compatible model and simulation, then restore the prognostic arrays:\n\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid,\n                   coriolis=FPlane(f=1e-4),\n                   stratification=ConstantStratification(N²=1e-5))\nsimulation = Simulation(model; Δt=10.0, stop_iteration=200,\n                        output=NetCDFOutput(path=\"output\",\n                                            schedule=IterationInterval(10)))\nrestore!(simulation, \"output/state0011.nc\")   # iteration 100\nrun!(simulation)                               # iterations 101 to 200\n\nrestore!(simulation, path) also sets the simulation clock to the file's time and iteration, so stop_time and stop_iteration count from the start of the original run, and output, energy diagnostics, and particles continue the original time axis. Continuing into the same directory appends state0012.nc onward without writing the restart state again. Restarting from an earlier file of a longer run needs overwrite=true, which replaces the later files. restore!(model, path) restores the fields only.\n\nThe restart dimensions, domain extent, wave formulation, and generalized-PV convention must match the receiving model; a different f0 or N² profile is allowed but warns, since it changes the flow inverted from q. Older snapshots without the metadata remain readable. Diagnostic arrays are reconstructed after the prognostic fields are copied onto the model's architecture. Particle positions are not stored in state files, so particles start new trajectories after a restart.","title":"Restart"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/io/#Failure-behavior","page":"I/O and Output","text":"An error while setting up output, such as state files from another run, is raised before anything is written and leaves the simulation Ready: run it again with another path or overwrite=true. Exceptions during the run, including while writing, move the simulation to Failed, close the state, diagnostics, and particle managers, and are rethrown. Use try/finally to guarantee cleanup:\n\ntry\n    run!(simulation)\nfinally\n    finalize_simulation!(simulation)\nend","title":"Failure behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/diagnostics/#diagnostics","page":"Diagnostics","text":"Diagnostics operate on the model that owns the fields and runtime metadata.","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/diagnostics/#Flow-kinetic-energy","page":"Diagnostics","text":"kinetic_energy = flow_kinetic_energy(model)\n\nThe result is a host scalar, also when the model runs on a GPU.","title":"Flow kinetic energy"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/diagnostics/#Wave-energy","page":"Diagnostics","text":"B_energy, A_energy = wave_energy(model)\n\nThe returned tuple reports the envelope and diagnosed-amplitude energies summed over the domain. The diagnostic reconstructs fields required by the selected wave formulation before reducing them.","title":"Wave energy"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/diagnostics/#Runtime-inspection","page":"Diagnostics","text":"simulation.clock.time\nsimulation.clock.iteration\nsimulation.state\narchitecture(simulation)\n\nUse an IterationInterval or TimeInterval when constructing the simulation to schedule energy diagnostics. verbose=true reports progress at the corresponding approximate iteration cadence.\n\nFor scheduled component time series, configure EnergyDiagnosticsOutput. See I/O and restart for the saved operator fields and FFTW analysis, or run examples/compute_energy.jl output/ to write spatial kinetic-energy fields.","title":"Runtime inspection"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#ybj-plus","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"The Young–Ben Jelloul framework evolves a complex near-inertial-wave envelope on the slow balanced-flow timescale.","title":"YBJ⁺ wave model"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#Wave-equation","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"QGYBJ+.jl advances B according to\n\npartial_t B + J(psiB)\n= -fraci2zeta B\n  -fraci f_02nabla_h^2 A\n  + mathcal D_B\n\nwhere zeta=nabla_h^2psi. The terms represent advection, refraction, dispersion, and configured dissipation.","title":"Wave equation"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#YBJ-relation","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"For YBJPlus(),\n\nB=L^+A\n=partial_zleft(fracf_0^2N^2partial_z Aright)\n +frac14nabla_h^2A\n\nAt horizontal wavenumber k_h this is\n\nwidehat B\n=partial_zleft(fracf_0^2N^2partial_zwidehat Aright)\n -frack_h^24widehat A\n\nThe Helmholtz term regularizes short horizontal scales. Recovering A requires one tridiagonal vertical solve per horizontal Fourier mode.","title":"YBJ⁺ relation"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#Original-YBJ-relation","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"For YBJ(), the Helmholtz term is omitted:\n\nB=partial_zleft(fracf_0^2N^2partial_z Aright)\n\nThe implementation recovers A by vertical integration with coefficient N^2f_0^2 and enforces the tendency solvability condition.","title":"Original YBJ relation"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#Depth-mean-part-of-the-envelope","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"With A_z=0 at the bottom and the lid, LA has zero depth mean, so the wave velocity e^-if_0tLA has no barotropic part. In the horizontally uniform column (k_h=0) both relations reduce to B=LA, so that column of B has zero depth mean; for YBJ() every column does. The model removes any other depth mean from B: from an initial condition (including the depth mean of a SurfaceWave profile, which is small for a surface layer much thinner than the ocean) and at every time step, where refraction would otherwise create it. A depth-uniform envelope is removed entirely, with a warning.\n\ninvert_B_to_A!(model)\nA = model.fields.A","title":"Depth-mean part of the envelope"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#Wave-vertical-velocity","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"The wave velocity is u+iv=e^-if_0tLA, and continuity gives its vertical velocity (Asselin & Young 2019, equation 2.10),\n\nw = -fracf_0^2N^2partial_s A_ze^-if_0t + textcc\nqquad\npartial_s = tfrac12(partial_x - ipartial_y)\n\ncompute_ybj_vertical_velocity!(model; t) evaluates it where A_z lives, on the faces between levels, with N^2 on the same faces. The face values then satisfy discrete continuity with the model's LA exactly, and vanish at the bottom and the lid, where A_z=0. As for the QG vertical velocity, the face values are averaged onto cell centres for storage. compute_total_velocities!(model; t) adds it, at the same phase, to the instantaneous velocity.\n\ncompute_ybj_vertical_velocity!(model; t=simulation.clock.time)\nw = model.fields.w","title":"Wave vertical velocity"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#Select-a-formulation","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"plus_model = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, formulation=YBJPlus())\nybj_model = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, formulation=YBJ())\npassive_model = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, formulation=PassiveWave())\n\nPassiveWave() retains envelope advection but omits refraction and dispersion. NoDispersion() disables dispersion while retaining the other processes of the selected YBJ formulation.","title":"Select a formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#Initialize-and-diagnose-waves","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"set!(model; waves=SurfaceWave(amplitude=0.1, scale=30.0))\nB_energy, A_energy = wave_energy(model)\n\nETD-RK2 evaluates the explicit wave tendency at both stages and integrates horizontal hyperdiffusion with its exponential factor.","title":"Initialize and diagnose waves"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/ybj_plus/#References","page":"YBJ+ Wave Model","text":"Young, W. R. & Ben Jelloul, M. (1997), “Propagation of near-inertial oscillations through a geostrophic flow,” Journal of Marine Research, 55, 735–766.\nAsselin, O. & Young, W. R. (2019), “An improved model of near-inertial wave dynamics”, Journal of Fluid Mechanics, 876, 428–448.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#concepts","page":"Key Concepts","text":"","title":"Key concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Ownership","page":"Key Concepts","text":"QGYBJ+.jl separates immutable geometry, model state, and run orchestration:\n\nRectilinearGrid → QGYBJModel → Simulation\n\nRectilinearGrid defines global coordinates and wavenumbers.\nQGYBJModel owns physical choices, numerical choices, fields and FFT resources on the grid's architecture, and optional particles.\nSimulation owns time, ETD-RK2, stopping criteria, output, and diagnostics.\n\nFinalize a simulation when the run ends. This closes its output services and releases runtime resources owned by the model.","title":"Ownership"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Model-fields","page":"Key Concepts","text":"All model arrays use (z, x, y) ordering.\n\nField Role\nq prognostic generalized PV; with feedback it contains balanced and wave contributions\nB prognostic complex near-inertial-wave envelope\npsi balanced streamfunction diagnosed from PV\nA, C wave amplitude and its vertical derivative\nu, v, w physical-space velocity components\n\nThe horizontal velocity follows u=-partial_ypsi and v=partial_xpsi. The relative vorticity is zeta=nabla_h^2psi.","title":"Model fields"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Wave-formulations","page":"Key Concepts","text":"Component Meaning\nYBJPlus regularized YBJ⁺ wave dynamics\nYBJ original Young–Ben Jelloul relation\nPassiveWave wave-envelope advection without refraction or dispersion\n\nThe model advances B and diagnoses A. See YBJ⁺ wave model for the relation between them.","title":"Wave formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Flow-and-feedback-choices","page":"Key Concepts","text":"The common configurations are:\n\nUse case Flow Feedback\nprescribed flow acting on waves FixedFlow() NoFeedback()\nevolving flow acting on waves EvolvingFlow() NoWaveFeedback()\ntwo-way wave–mean coupling EvolvingFlow() WaveMeanFeedback()\n\nWith two-way coupling, wave PV modifies streamfunction inversion. In all cases, advection and refraction depend on the selected flow and wave formulation.","title":"Flow and feedback choices"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Coordinates-and-transforms","page":"Key Concepts","text":"Horizontal boundaries are periodic.\nThe vertical coordinate increases from the bottom to z=0 at the surface.\nVertical nodes are cell centered.\nSpectral fields are complex; diagnosed velocities are real.\nHorizontal derivatives are spectral, while nonlinear products are formed in physical space.\n\nContinue with configuration or the physics overview.","title":"Coordinates and transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#api-index","page":"API Map","text":"Frequently used exported entry points are grouped below. Detailed signatures and examples are on the linked API pages.","title":"API map"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Construction","page":"API Map","text":"RectilinearGrid\nQGYBJModel\nSimulation\nNetCDFOutput\nEnergyDiagnosticsOutput","title":"Construction"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Initialization","page":"API Map","text":"set!\nset_mean_flow!\nset_surface_waves!\nset_exponential_surface_waves!\nset_wave_packet!\nFieldArray\nFieldFile\nrestore!","title":"Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Integration-and-lifecycle","page":"API Map","text":"ExponentialRungeKutta2\nstep!\nrun!\nfinalize_model!\nfinalize_simulation!","title":"Integration and lifecycle"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Operators-and-diagnostics","page":"API Map","text":"invert_q_to_psi!\ninvert_B_to_A!\ncompute_velocities!\ncompute_vertical_velocity!\ncompute_ybj_vertical_velocity!\ncompute_total_velocities!\ncompute_wave_velocities!\nflow_kinetic_energy\nwave_energy","title":"Operators and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Architectures","page":"API Map","text":"CPU\nGPU\narchitecture\non_architecture","title":"Architectures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Particles","page":"API Map","text":"ParticleConfig\nParticleConfig3D\ninitialize_particles!\nadvect_particles!\nparticles_in_box\nparticles_in_circle\nparticles_in_grid_3d\nparticles_in_layers\nparticles_random_3d\nparticles_custom\nParticleOutputManager","title":"Particles"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#qg-equations","page":"QG Equations","text":"","title":"QG equations"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#Generalized-potential-vorticity","page":"QG Equations","text":"The prognostic field is the total generalized PV,\n\nq = q^g + q^w\n\nwith balanced component\n\nq^g = nabla_h^2psi\n    + partial_zleft(fracf_0^2N^2(z)partial_zpsiright)\n\nFor constant stratification this becomes\n\nq^g = nabla_h^2psi\n    + fracf_0^2N^2partial_z^2psi\n\nWhen the flow evolves,\n\npartial_t q + J(psiq) = mathcal D_q\nqquad\nJ(ab)=partial_xapartial_yb-partial_yapartial_xb\n\nmathcal D_q denotes the configured horizontal and vertical dissipation. A FixedFlow() model holds the balanced flow fixed instead of advancing this equation.","title":"Generalized potential vorticity"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#Wave-contribution","page":"QG Equations","text":"With WaveMeanFeedback(), the dimensional wave envelope contributes\n\nq^w = fraci2f_0J(B^*B)\n    + frac14f_0nabla_h^2B^2\n\nThe factors of 1f_0 are part of the dimensional equation. With NoFeedback() or NoWaveFeedback(), the solver uses q^w=0.\n\nThe prognostic q is then the total PV, but the flow's dissipation mathcal D_q acts on the balanced PV q-q^w only: q^w belongs to the waves, which have their own damping. Damping the total PV instead would create a balanced flow from the wave PV and could add energy. The time stepper keeps the exact damping factor on q and adds the damping of q^w to the explicit part, so modes whose damping is very stiff relax to q = q^w.\n\nFor YBJPlus(), this is the wave part of the \"XV^+\" potential vorticity of Asselin & Young (2019), equation (3.5), with B=L^+A. For the original YBJ() formulation the prognostic envelope is instead B=LA, recovering the original Xie–Vanneste PV. A&Y reach (3.5) from that PV by the substitution Lmapsto L^+, and that substitution is what gives the YBJ^+` coupled system its \"coupled energy\" conservation law (their equations (3.6)–(3.7)).\n\nNote the intended contrast with wave kinetic energy, which uses LA: A&Y equation (4.7) and the remark that \"to define WKE for YBJ^+ we use L, not L^+\".","title":"Wave contribution"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#Streamfunction-inversion","page":"QG Equations","text":"The streamfunction is diagnosed from balanced PV:\n\nnabla_h^2psi\n+ partial_zleft(fracf_0^2N^2partial_zpsiright)\n= q-q^w\n\nWave PV is subtracted only for inversion. The prognostic total q is restored before each ETD-RK2 stage continues.\n\nAfter horizontal Fourier transformation, every (k_xk_y) mode is a tridiagonal vertical problem,\n\n-k_h^2widehatpsi\n+ partial_zleft(a(z)partial_zwidehatpsiright)\n= widehatq-q^w\nqquad a(z)=fracf_0^2N^2(z)\n\nThe public inversion entry point is:\n\ninvert_q_to_psi!(model)","title":"Streamfunction inversion"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#Velocity","page":"QG Equations","text":"Balanced horizontal velocity and relative vorticity follow from\n\nu=-partial_ypsi\nqquad\nv=partial_xpsi\nqquad\nzeta=nabla_h^2psi\n\nUse model-level operators so transforms, work arrays, and the architecture are selected from the owning runtime:\n\ncompute_velocities!(model)\ncompute_vertical_velocity!(model)\n\nHorizontal derivatives and Jacobians are pseudo-spectral and use two-thirds dealiasing. Vertical operators use second-order differences. See numerical methods.","title":"Velocity"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#Vertical-velocity","page":"QG Equations","text":"The balanced flow carries an ageostrophic vertical velocity. Eliminating the time derivatives between the vorticity and buoyancy equations,\n\npartial_tzeta + J(psizeta) = f_0partial_z w\nqquad\npartial_t b + J(psib) + N^2 w = 0\nqquad\nb = f_0partial_zpsi\n\ngives the omega equation,\n\nN^2nabla_h^2 w + f_0^2partial_z^2 w\n= 2nablacdotmathbf Q\n= 2f_0leftJ(psi_zzeta) - J(psi_xpsi_xz) - J(psi_ypsi_yz)right\n\nwith w=0 at the bottom and the rigid lid. compute_vertical_velocity! solves it on the faces between levels, where the model's buoyancy f_0delta_zpsi lives, with N^2 sampled on the faces. The discrete forcing is the one implied by the flux-form PV inversion, so the diagnosed w satisfies the model's own discrete vorticity and buoyancy equations exactly.\n\nWith wave feedback the conserved PV is q^g+q^w, so the vorticity equation gains -D_t q^w and the omega equation the forcing f_0partial_z(D_t q^w), where D_t q^w = partial_t q^w + J(psiq^w) and partial_t q^w follows from the inviscid wave tendency. The w of a coupled run then closes the coupled buoyancy equation exactly. As psi is the Lagrangian-mean streamfunction (Xie & Vanneste 2015; Wagner & Young 2016), u, v, and w are Lagrangian-mean velocities; see Wave–mean interaction for the instantaneous velocity.\n\nThe vertical velocity is a diagnostic: QG advection of q is horizontal, so w does not feed back on the flow. It is computed on demand, by compute_velocities!(model), compute_vertical_velocity!(model), and NetCDF output with velocities; a time step leaves w = 0. Particles move horizontally and do not use it.\n\nThe face values are averaged onto the cell centres for storage, so u, v, and w share one grid in output files. The wave-induced vertical velocity of compute_ybj_vertical_velocity! is stored the same way (see YBJ⁺ wave model).","title":"Vertical velocity"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/qg_equations/#References","page":"QG Equations","text":"Xie, J.-H. & Vanneste, J. (2015), “A generalised-Lagrangian-mean model of the interactions between near-inertial waves and mean flow”, Journal of Fluid Mechanics, 774, 143–169.\nVallis, G. K. (2017), Atmospheric and Oceanic Fluid Dynamics.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/particles/#particles","page":"Particle Advection","text":"Particle positions and tracker metadata are optional model-owned data.","title":"Particle advection"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/particles/#Install-particles-on-a-model","page":"Particle Advection","text":"configuration = ParticleConfig{Float64}(\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces),\n    x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces),\n    y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    z_level=-500.0,\n    nx_particles=32,\n    ny_particles=32,\n    interpolation_method=TRICUBIC,\n)\ninitialize_particles!(model, configuration)\n\nThe tracker is available as model.particles. Its geometry, FFT plans, and velocity source all refer back to the owning model. Particles move with the horizontal velocity and keep their depth.\n\nPositions and velocities have the configuration's precision (Float32 for the particles_in_* helpers unless given), while the particle time is always Float64, so that the times of long runs stay exact.\n\nParticle positions, the velocity that advects them, and the advection itself live on the model's architecture: on a GPU, a particle step copies the model's horizontal velocity within the device and advances every particle in one kernel, with no transfer to the host. Copy the positions to use them on the host, Array(model.particles.particles.x); particle output and the in-memory history do so when they record.\n\nThe wave_motion option of the particle configuration selects how particles feel the near-inertial waves:\n\n:averaged (default): phase-averaged trajectories, advected by the Lagrangian-mean velocity nabla^perppsi. Because psi is the Lagrangian-mean streamfunction, this already includes the waves' Stokes drift.\n:instantaneous: trajectories that trace the inertial oscillation, advected by the wave velocity e^-if_0tLA, with the phase evaluated at every integration stage, plus the Lagrangian mean less the Stokes drift that the horizontal orbits make up themselves. Their drift averaged over an inertial period is therefore again the Lagrangian mean. The part of the Stokes drift that comes from vertical excursions in the vertical shear, which particles at a fixed depth do not make, stays in their mean velocity. The particle time step must resolve the inertial period, f_0Delta t lesssim 02.\n\nconfig = particles_in_box(-500.0; x_max=Lx, y_max=Ly, wave_motion=:instantaneous)\n\nBoth velocity components are evaluated at the cell centres and interpolated to the particle positions with interpolation_method: TRILINEAR, TRICUBIC (default), or QUINTIC. See Particle interpolation. use_3d_advection was removed: setting it to true raises an error.","title":"Install particles on a model"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/particles/#Distributions-over-depth","page":"Particle Advection","text":"Particles released at several depths each move at their own depth.\n\nlayers = particles_in_layers(\n    [-100.0, -500.0, -1000.0];\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces),\n    x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces),\n    y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    nx=16,\n    ny=16,\n)\ninitialize_particles!(model, layers)\n\nOther helpers create uniform volumes, circles, random clouds, and explicit custom positions.","title":"Distributions over depth"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/particles/#Integration","page":"Particle Advection","text":"Particles installed on a model are advanced automatically by run!(simulation), after each model step. A direct update moves them from current_time to current_time + Δt and takes the model's current state as the velocity at the end of that interval, so advance the model over the same interval first:\n\ntimestepper = ExponentialRungeKutta2(Δt=10.0)\nfor n in 0:99\n    step!(model, timestepper)                          # fields: t → t + Δt\n    advect_particles!(model, 10.0; current_time=10.0n) # particles: t → t + Δt\nend\n\nintegration_method selects :euler, :rk2 (default), or :rk4. The model's velocity is known only at the ends of a step, so the tracker keeps the velocity from the end of the previous step, and every integration stage interpolates linearly in time between the start and the end of the step. In an evolving flow :rk2 and :rk4 are then second-order accurate in time, like the model's ETD-RK2 flow, and :euler is first-order. :rk4 reduces only the error from trajectories that curve within a step, at twice the cost of :rk2. The first manual step has no earlier velocity and uses the end-of-step velocity throughout; run! records the initial velocity before its first step.","title":"Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/particles/#Boundaries","page":"Particle Advection","text":"Horizontal boundaries are periodic (periodic_x and periodic_y, both on by default; a non-periodic direction clamps particles into the domain). Particles keep their depth. Vertically, the interpolation mirrors the horizontal velocity at the lid and the bottom, where the rigid-lid boundary conditions give it zero vertical derivative, so particles near either boundary see a smooth velocity.","title":"Boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/particles/#Trajectories","page":"Particle Advection","text":"Attach a manager to the simulation for automatic initial, scheduled, and final particle output:\n\nparticle_output = ParticleOutputManager(\n    \"output\";\n    save_interval_iter=20,\n    output_mode=:trajectory,\n)\nsimulation = Simulation(model; particle_output=particle_output, ...)\n\nGive a schedule: save_interval_iter iterations, or save_interval_time in the model's time units (seconds for a dimensional model). There is no default, and the iteration interval wins when both are given. The output modes are:\n\n:trajectory: one file, written when the run ends;\n:streaming: one file, extended at every save, so a run that is stopped early keeps the trajectories saved so far, without holding them in memory;\n:snapshots: one file per save.\n\nEach record holds the particle positions and their horizontal velocities, u and v, at the recorded time. A run never silently replaces existing particle files of the same mode and prefix: pass overwrite=true to the manager to replace them. Particle positions are not part of the model's restart files, so particles start new trajectories after a restart.\n\nWithout a manager, a tracker can keep its own in-memory history of the positions, written with write_particle_trajectories. It is off by default; set save_interval in the particle configuration (0 for every step) and, if needed, max_save_points (1000 by default; the tracker warns when it is reached).","title":"Trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"api/timestepping/#api-timestepping","page":"Time Stepping","text":"QGYBJ+.jl uses second-order exponential Runge–Kutta integration exclusively. Horizontal hyperdiffusion is handled by exact integrating factors; the remaining tendency is evaluated in two stages.","title":"Time integration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/timestepping/#Simulation-owned-stepping","page":"Time Stepping","text":"simulation = Simulation(model; Δt=10.0, stop_iteration=100, output=false)\nrun!(simulation)\n\nThe timestepper and reusable workspace live at simulation.timestepper.","title":"Simulation-owned stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"api/timestepping/#Manual-stepping","page":"Time Stepping","text":"timestepper = ExponentialRungeKutta2(Δt=10.0)\nstep!(model, timestepper)\n\nThe first step allocates an ExponentialRungeKutta2Workspace; later steps reuse it. Manual stepping advances fields only; run! also manages time, particles, output, diagnostics, and failure handling.","title":"Manual stepping"},{"category":"type","location":"api/timestepping/#QGYBJplus.ExponentialRungeKutta2","page":"Time Stepping","text":"Second-order exponential Runge-Kutta timestepper.\n\nworkspace starts as nothing and is filled with an ExponentialRungeKutta2Workspace on the first step!, which is when the field shapes are first known. A mutable field cannot be narrowed after that assignment, so it stays untyped; step! re-establishes the concrete type with an isa check before entering the kernels, and the field is read once per step rather than inside any loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ExponentialRungeKutta2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/timestepping/#QGYBJplus.ExponentialRungeKutta2Workspace","page":"Time Stepping","text":"ExponentialRungeKutta2Workspace(fields, plans=nothing; G=nothing)\n\nReusable storage for model-owned step!: the first-stage values of q and B and their first-stage tendencies. Allocate it once per simulation; step! reuses it on every later step.\n\nA step computes its stage diagnostics (ψ, velocities, A) in the model's own diagnostic fields, which the end of the step recomputes from the new q and B. The second-stage tendencies are formed in scratch buffers and enter the update directly, which writes q and B in place. Until that final kernel q and B are only read, so a step that throws leaves them unchanged (the diagnostic fields may then hold stage values).\n\nPass the model's runtime geometry as G to tie the workspace to that model: step! then replaces it rather than reuse it for a different model, even one whose fields have the same shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ExponentialRungeKutta2Workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/timestepping/#QGYBJplus.step!","page":"Time Stepping","text":"Advance model by one model-owned exponential Runge-Kutta step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/timestepping/#QGYBJplus.run!","page":"Time Stepping","text":"Advance a configured simulation with the current ETD-RK2 driver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.run!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#configuration","page":"Configuration","text":"Build a model by composing typed physical and numerical choices.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Geometry","page":"Configuration","text":"grid = RectilinearGrid(\n    CPU();\n    size=(128, 128, 64),\n    x=(-250e3, 250e3),\n    y=(-250e3, 250e3),\n    z=(-4000.0, 0.0),\n)\n\nAlternatively, provide extent=(Lx, Ly, Lz) and optionally centered=true. The first argument selects the architecture: CPU() (the default when omitted) or GPU() after using CUDA; see CPU and GPU execution.","title":"Geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Physics-choices","page":"Configuration","text":"model = QGYBJModel(\n    grid=grid,\n    coriolis=FPlane(f=1e-4),\n    stratification=ConstantStratification(N²=1e-5),\n    flow=EvolvingFlow(),\n    feedback=WaveMeanFeedback(),\n    formulation=YBJPlus(),\n)\n\nAvailable choices include:\n\nConstructor keyword Values\nflow FixedFlow(), EvolvingFlow()\nfeedback NoFeedback(), NoWaveFeedback(), WaveMeanFeedback()\nformulation YBJPlus(), YBJ(), PassiveWave()\nlinear NonlinearDynamics(), LinearDynamics()\nno_dispersion Dispersive(), NoDispersion()\ninviscid Dissipative(), Inviscid()\n\nSee key concepts for the recommended flow and feedback combinations.","title":"Physics choices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Numerical-choices","page":"Configuration","text":"closure = HorizontalHyperdiffusivity(\n    flow=FlowHyperdiffusivity(coefficient=0),\n    wave=WaveHyperdiffusivity(coefficient=1e5),\n)\n\nmodel = QGYBJModel(\n    grid=grid,\n    closure=closure,\n    vertical_diffusion=VerticalDiffusivity(coefficient=0),\n)\n\nThis leaves the balanced flow undamped and applies one fourth-order damping term to waves. Each order is the total derivative order.\n\nThe default closure, HorizontalHyperdiffusivity(), keeps the coefficients of nondimensional setups, which damp nothing at dimensional wavenumbers; a nonlinear model warns when that is so. For a dimensional run, choose the coefficients from the grid spacing and time step:\n\nν = compute_hyperdiff_coeff(dx=grid.dx, dy=grid.dy, dt=Δt, order=8)\nclosure = HorizontalHyperdiffusivity(\n    flow=FlowHyperdiffusivity(coefficient=ν, order=8),\n    wave=WaveHyperdiffusivity(coefficient=ν, order=8),\n)\n\ncompute_hyperdiff_coeff damps the largest wavenumber the two-thirds dealiasing keeps by one e-fold every efold_steps=10 steps; pass inviscid=Inviscid() to run without horizontal damping deliberately.","title":"Numerical choices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Execution-choices","page":"Configuration","text":"Where the model runs is fixed by the grid's architecture. Time integration and output belong to a separate simulation:\n\nsimulation = Simulation(\n    model;\n    Δt=10.0,\n    stop_iteration=1000,\n    output=NetCDFOutput(\n        path=\"output\",\n        schedule=IterationInterval(100),\n    ),\n)\n\nOnly ETD-RK2 is available; constructing Simulation installs an ExponentialRungeKutta2 timestepper automatically.","title":"Execution choices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#initial-conditions","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Model-level setters update model-owned arrays and use runtime transform plans automatically.","title":"Initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Analytical-mean-flow","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"set!(\n    model;\n    ψ=(x, y, z) -> 1e3 * sin(2π * x / model.grid.extent[1]),\n    pv_method=:qg,\n)\n\nUse pv_method=:barotropic for an imposed vertically uniform flow, as in the Asselin dipole example.","title":"Analytical mean flow"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Mean-flow-from-vorticity","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"The mean flow can be given by its relative vorticity instead of its streamfunction:\n\nLx, Ly = model.grid.extent[1], model.grid.extent[2]\nset!(model; ζ=(x, y, z) -> 1e-5 * sin(2π * x / Lx) * cos(2π * y / Ly))\nset!(model; ζ=FieldFile(\"eddies.jld2\", \"zeta\"))\n\nζ (or zeta) takes a function of (x, y, z), an array, a FieldArray, or a FieldFile. The streamfunction follows by inverting nabla_h^2 psi = zeta at each level, and pv_method then builds the potential vorticity from ψ as it does for a given ψ. The horizontal mean of ζ at each level is dropped, since the circulation around a doubly periodic domain vanishes; the model warns when that mean exceeds 1% of the field's rms, as it does for a single eddy cut out of a larger region.","title":"Mean flow from vorticity"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Deterministic-random-mean-flow","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"set!(\n    model;\n    mean_flow=RandomStreamfunction(\n        amplitude=0.4,\n        spectral_slope=-3,\n        seed=42,\n    ),\n)\n\nEach Fourier coefficient is generated from a hash of its own indices, so a fixed seed gives the same field on every architecture, up to rounding in the GPU math library.","title":"Deterministic random mean flow"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Surface-confined-waves","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"set!(\n    model;\n    waves=SurfaceWave(\n        amplitude=0.1,\n        scale=30.0,\n        profile=:gaussian,\n    ),\n)\n\nThe profile may be :gaussian or :exponential, decaying over scale downward from the top of the domain, its rigid lid. The wave is horizontally uniform, and its depth mean is removed, since the wave velocity has no barotropic part (see YBJ⁺ wave model). For a surface layer much thinner than the ocean that changes the profile very little; for a layer comparable to the depth, it adds a weak opposite flow below the layer.","title":"Surface-confined waves"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Wave-packets","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"set_wave_packet!(\n    model;\n    amplitude=0.1,\n    kx=2,\n    ky=1,\n    sigma_k=0.5,\n)\n\nThe packet's Gaussian vertical envelope is centered z_center below the top of the domain, with width z_width; they default to half and a quarter of the depth.","title":"Wave packets"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Direct-array-access","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Pass a global (z, x, y) array directly to initialize a physical field:\n\nset!(model; ψ=psi_values, pv_method=:qg)\nset!(model; B=wave_values)\n\nUse FieldArray to declare spectral data or (x, y, z) layout:\n\nset!(model; B=FieldArray(B_hat; space=:spectral, layout=:zxy))\n\nFile-backed fields use FieldFile, which reads NetCDF, or JLD2 when the name ends in .jld2. Its default layout is the file-oriented (x, y, z) ordering:\n\nset!(model; B=FieldFile(\"initial.nc\", \"B\"))\n\nPublic setters rebuild dependent q, A, C, and velocity diagnostics.","title":"Direct array access"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/initial_conditions/#Fields-on-another-grid","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"A physical field given on another rectilinear grid is interpolated onto the model's grid when the coordinates of its points are known. A FieldFile finds them as the vectors x, y, and z (or depth) stored beside the field, read with the vertical conventions of stratification files; a FieldArray takes them as keywords, with z positive upward:\n\nset!(model; ζ=FieldFile(\"eddies.jld2\", \"zeta\"))\nset!(model; ψ=FieldFile(\"coarse_run.nc\", \"psi\"))\nset!(model; ζ=FieldArray(ζ_data; layout=:xyz, x=xs, y=ys, z=zs))\n\ngroup selects the NetCDF group, or the JLD2 group, NamedTuple, or Dict, that holds the field and its coordinates, as in FieldFile(\"input.jld2\", \"zeta\"; group=\"eddies\"). A stratification profile N2 kept in the same place shares the field's vertical coordinate, so one file can hold both:\n\nprofile = load_stratification_from_file(\"input.jld2\")   # z (or depth) and N2\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, stratification=profile)\nset!(model; ζ=FieldFile(\"input.jld2\", \"zeta\"))           # x, y, z, and zeta\n\nInterpolation is linear along each axis. In x and y it is periodic with the model's domain, so the data must hold one period of a periodic field, in any horizontal frame; a last point one period after the first is taken to repeat it. The model warns when the data leave a gap of more than a grid spacing at the periodic boundary, as for a region cut out of a larger domain, which also makes the field jump across that boundary. Vertically, the values are held beyond the data, with the same warning as for stratification profiles. Data at the model's own points are copied unchanged. Each point takes the value at its location rather than an average around it, so smooth data on a much finer grid to the model's resolution first. Spectral fields must be given on the model's grid.","title":"Fields on another grid"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/wave_mean/#wave-mean","page":"Wave-Mean Interaction","text":"Balanced flow advects and refracts near-inertial waves. Two-way coupling adds the wave contribution to generalized PV.","title":"Wave–mean interaction"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/wave_mean/#Choose-the-coupling","page":"Wave-Mean Interaction","text":"Use case Components\nprescribed flow acting on waves FixedFlow(), NoFeedback()\nevolving flow acting on waves EvolvingFlow(), NoWaveFeedback()\ntwo-way coupling EvolvingFlow(), WaveMeanFeedback()\n\nFor example:\n\nmodel = QGYBJModel(\n    grid=grid,\n    flow=EvolvingFlow(),\n    feedback=WaveMeanFeedback(),\n    formulation=YBJPlus(),\n)\n\nFixedFlow() always prevents balanced-flow evolution, so it also disables wave feedback: combining it with WaveMeanFeedback() warns and runs without feedback. Both no-feedback components omit wave PV from the inversion; NoWaveFeedback() makes the one-way evolving-flow intent explicit.","title":"Choose the coupling"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/wave_mean/#Wave-PV","page":"Wave-Mean Interaction","text":"For two-way coupling,\n\nq^w = fraci2f_0J(B^*B)\n    + frac14f_0nabla_h^2B^2\n\nHere YBJPlus() supplies B=L^+A (the XV^+ PV), while YBJ() supplies B=LA (the original XV PV).\n\nEach ETD-RK2 stage diagnoses q^w from its stage-local complex wave field. Streamfunction inversion uses q-q^w; the prognostic total q is never modified.","title":"Wave PV"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/wave_mean/#Mean-and-wave-velocities","page":"Wave-Mean Interaction","text":"The balanced streamfunction is that of the Lagrangian-mean flow (Xie & Vanneste 2015, §2; Wagner & Young 2016, eq. 3.22): nabla^perppsi already contains the waves' Stokes drift mathbf u^S. The velocity at a fixed point adds the near-inertial oscillation to the Eulerian mean,\n\nu + iv = (u^L + iv^L) - (u^S + iv^S) + e^-if_0tLA\n\nand its vertical velocity combines the mean w^L, -w^S, and the wave vertical velocity. Three calls give these fields:\n\ncompute_velocities!(model)                       # Lagrangian mean\ncompute_total_velocities!(model; t)              # instantaneous, at time t\ncompute_wave_velocities!(model; include_wave_velocity=false)  # mean → Eulerian mean\n\nParticles default to phase-averaged trajectories, advected by the Lagrangian mean; wave_motion=:instantaneous follows the inertial oscillation instead (see Particle advection).","title":"Mean and wave velocities"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/wave_mean/#Compare-coupled-and-uncoupled-runs","page":"Wave-Mean Interaction","text":"Construct separate models with identical geometry and initial conditions but different feedback components. Each model owns independent arrays and runtime resources, so finalize each simulation independently.\n\nflow_energy = flow_kinetic_energy(model)\nB_energy, A_energy = wave_energy(model)\n\nIn the inviscid continuous system, wave–mean exchange is internal to the combined energy budget. Dissipation, timestep, and spatial resolution control the corresponding discrete error.\n\nSee Xie & Vanneste (2015) for the generalized-Lagrangian-mean derivation and the QG equations for inversion details.","title":"Compare coupled and uncoupled runs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#api-types","page":"Core Types","text":"","title":"Core types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Ownership","page":"Core Types","text":"RectilinearGrid is immutable. QGYBJModel owns model data and ephemeral runtime resources. Simulation separately owns execution lifecycle.","title":"Ownership"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Architectures","page":"Core Types","text":"See CPU and GPU execution.","title":"Architectures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Physical-components","page":"Core Types","text":"","title":"Physical components"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Numerical-components","page":"Core Types","text":"","title":"Numerical components"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Initialization-and-scheduling","page":"Core Types","text":"","title":"Initialization and scheduling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.RectilinearGrid","page":"Core Types","text":"RectilinearGrid([architecture=CPU()], [FT=Float64]; size, extent=nothing,\n                x=nothing, y=nothing, z=nothing, centered=false)\n\nImmutable owner of the model's global rectilinear geometry. Horizontal coordinates are periodic Fourier collocation nodes on half-open intervals; vertical coordinates are cell centers between z_faces.\n\nProvide size=(nx, ny, nz) together with either extent=(Lx, Ly, Lz) or explicit bounds x=(x₁, x₂), y=(y₁, y₂), and z=(z₁, z₂). When only extent is supplied, the horizontal origin is zero (or centered when centered=true) and the vertical interval is (-Lz, 0). The kx and ky properties use FFTW's spectral ordering.\n\narchitecture selects where a model built on this grid stores and computes its fields: CPU() (default) or GPU(). The coordinate vectors held by the grid itself always live on the host. A GPU backend is stored as its arrays report it (see array_architecture).\n\nFT is the floating-point type of the grid and of every array of a model built on it. Float32 halves the model's memory and is much faster on most GPUs; see the performance page for its accuracy.\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(GPU(); size=(128, 128, 64), extent=(1e5, 1e5, 4e3))\ngrid32 = RectilinearGrid(GPU(), Float32; size=(256, 256, 128), extent=(1e5, 1e5, 4e3))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.RectilinearGrid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.ModelFields","page":"Core Types","text":"ModelFields{T, RT, CT}\n\nPrognostic and diagnostic arrays owned by a QG-YBJ model. All fields use (z, x, y) dimension order. The prognostic fields q and B, together with the diagnostic fields psi, A, and C, are spectral and complex-valued; the velocity fields u, v, and w are real-valued.\n\nRT and CT are the array types of the model's architecture: Array on the CPU, or the GPU package's device array (e.g. CuArray) on a GPU.\n\nstep! updates these arrays in place, so references to the container and to its individual arrays both stay live across steps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ModelFields"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.QGYBJModel","page":"Core Types","text":"QGYBJModel\n\nComposition-first owner of immutable geometry, typed physics and numerics, model fields, and ephemeral runtime resources.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.QGYBJModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.ModelPhysics","page":"Core Types","text":"Focused physical choices owned by a QGYBJModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ModelPhysics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.ModelNumerics","page":"Core Types","text":"Focused numerical choices owned by a QGYBJModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ModelNumerics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.ModelRuntime","page":"Core Types","text":"ModelRuntime\n\nEphemeral execution resources associated with a model: its architecture, kernel-facing geometry, transform plans, scratch workspace, operator coefficients, and dealiasing mask.\n\ncoefficients holds the host profiles (written to output files and returned to users); device_coefficients holds the same profiles stored on the architecture, which is what the kernels read. On the CPU both refer to the same vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ModelRuntime"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.OperatorCoefficients","page":"Core Types","text":"Precomputed vertical coefficients used by model operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.OperatorCoefficients"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.Simulation","page":"Core Types","text":"Simulation\n\nLifecycle owner for a model run. The simulation composes a model, clock, typed timestepper, stop criteria, scheduling/output configuration, and state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.Simulation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.Clock","page":"Core Types","text":"Mutable simulation clock owned by a Simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.Clock"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.SimulationState","page":"Core Types","text":"Lifecycle states for a Simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.SimulationState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.CPU","page":"Core Types","text":"CPU()\n\nRun on the host with Array storage. Kernels use KernelAbstractions' CPU backend, which is multithreaded when Julia is started with several threads, and horizontal transforms use FFTW.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.CPU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.GPU","page":"Core Types","text":"GPU(backend)\nGPU()\n\nRun on a single GPU described by a KernelAbstractions backend. GPU() uses the default backend of the loaded GPU package: after using CUDA this is CUDA.CUDABackend(), with cuFFT for the horizontal transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.GPU"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.architecture","page":"Core Types","text":"architecture(x)\n\nArchitecture that owns x: an array, a KernelAbstractions backend, a grid, a model, or a simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.architecture"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.on_architecture","page":"Core Types","text":"on_architecture(architecture, x)\n\nReturn x with every array it contains stored on architecture. Arrays that are already there are returned as they are, without a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.on_architecture"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.FPlane","page":"Core Types","text":"Constant Coriolis parameter for an f-plane model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.FPlane"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.ConstantStratification","page":"Core Types","text":"Constant buoyancy frequency squared N² in s⁻².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ConstantStratification"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.FixedFlow","page":"Core Types","text":"Hold the balanced flow fixed while the wave field evolves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.FixedFlow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.EvolvingFlow","page":"Core Types","text":"Evolve the balanced flow together with the wave field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.EvolvingFlow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.NoFeedback","page":"Core Types","text":"Omit wave potential-vorticity feedback from the balanced-flow inversion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.NoFeedback"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.NoWaveFeedback","page":"Core Types","text":"Express one-way flow-to-wave coupling without wave PV feedback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.NoWaveFeedback"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.WaveMeanFeedback","page":"Core Types","text":"Enable bidirectional wave–mean-flow coupling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.WaveMeanFeedback"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.YBJPlus","page":"Core Types","text":"Use the elliptically regularized YBJ⁺ wave formulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.YBJPlus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.YBJ","page":"Core Types","text":"Use the original Young–Ben Jelloul wave formulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.YBJ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.PassiveWave","page":"Core Types","text":"Advect the wave envelope as a passive field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.PassiveWave"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.HorizontalHyperdiffusivity","page":"Core Types","text":"HorizontalHyperdiffusivity(; flow, wave)\n\nHorizontal damping composed from explicit balanced-flow and wave components.\n\nThe no-argument constructor, also the model's default closure, keeps the fourth- and twelfth-order terms of nondimensional setups: coefficients (0.01, 10.0) for the flow and (0.0, 10.0) for the waves. With dimensional wavenumbers (kₕ ~ 10⁻⁴ m⁻¹) they damp nothing, so a dimensional run should give its own coefficients, for instance from compute_hyperdiff_coeff:\n\nν = compute_hyperdiff_coeff(dx=grid.dx, dy=grid.dy, dt=Δt, order=8)\nclosure = HorizontalHyperdiffusivity(flow=FlowHyperdiffusivity(coefficient=ν, order=8),\n                                     wave=WaveHyperdiffusivity(coefficient=ν, order=8))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.HorizontalHyperdiffusivity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.FlowHyperdiffusivity","page":"Core Types","text":"FlowHyperdiffusivity(; coefficient, order=4)\n\nHorizontal damping for the balanced flow only. order is the total derivative order and must be positive and even. The wave field is not damped by this closure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.FlowHyperdiffusivity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.WaveHyperdiffusivity","page":"Core Types","text":"WaveHyperdiffusivity(; coefficient, order=4)\n\nHorizontal damping for the wave field only. order is the total derivative order and must be positive and even. The balanced flow is not damped by this closure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.WaveHyperdiffusivity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.VerticalDiffusivity","page":"Core Types","text":"Vertical diffusivity for balanced potential vorticity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.VerticalDiffusivity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.Dissipative","page":"Core Types","text":"Apply the configured horizontal and vertical dissipative closures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.Dissipative"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.Inviscid","page":"Core Types","text":"Disable all dissipative terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.Inviscid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.NonlinearDynamics","page":"Core Types","text":"Retain nonlinear advection and wave–flow interactions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.NonlinearDynamics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.LinearDynamics","page":"Core Types","text":"Disable nonlinear advection terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.LinearDynamics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.Dispersive","page":"Core Types","text":"Retain wave dispersion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.Dispersive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.NoDispersion","page":"Core Types","text":"Disable wave dispersion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.NoDispersion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.set!","page":"Core Types","text":"set!(owner; ψ, ζ, mean_flow, pv_method=:qg, waves, B, particles)\n\nSet declarative initial conditions on a model or its simulation.\n\nThe mean flow is given by its streamfunction ψ (or psi, or mean_flow, which also takes a RandomStreamfunction) or by its relative vorticity ζ (or zeta), from which ψ follows by inverting ∇ₕ²ψ = ζ at each level. pv_method selects how the potential vorticity follows from ψ: :qg, :barotropic, or :none. B (or waves, which also takes a SurfaceWave) sets the wave envelope. ψ and ζ may be functions of (x, y, z); all three may be arrays on the model's grid, FieldArrays, or FieldFiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.set!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.SurfaceWave","page":"Core Types","text":"Horizontally uniform, surface-confined wave initial condition, whose profile decays over scale downward from the top of the domain (its rigid lid). Its depth mean is removed when it is set, since the wave velocity has no barotropic part.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.SurfaceWave"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.RandomStreamfunction","page":"Core Types","text":"Deterministic random streamfunction spectrum for model initialization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.RandomStreamfunction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.FieldArray","page":"Core Types","text":"FieldArray(values; space=:physical, layout=:zxy, x=nothing, y=nothing, z=nothing)\n\nA three-dimensional field supplied directly to set!, on the model's grid or, when the coordinate vectors x, y, and z (positive upward) of its points are given, on any rectilinear grid, from which it is interpolated onto the model's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.FieldArray"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.FieldFile","page":"Core Types","text":"FieldFile(path, variable; space=:physical, layout=:xyz, group=nothing)\n\nA three-dimensional field supplied to set!, read from a NetCDF file, or from a JLD2 file when path ends in .jld2. group names the NetCDF group, or the JLD2 group, NamedTuple, or Dict, that holds variable. When the same place holds coordinate vectors x, y, and z (or depth), a field on another grid is interpolated onto the model's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.FieldFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.TimeInterval","page":"Core Types","text":"Schedule output or diagnostics at a time interval measured in seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.TimeInterval"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.IterationInterval","page":"Core Types","text":"Schedule output or diagnostics every interval model iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.IterationInterval"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.NetCDFOutput","page":"Core Types","text":"NetCDFOutput(; path=\"output\", schedule=nothing, fields=(:ψ, :waves), velocities=false, overwrite=false)\n\nDeclarative NetCDF output settings. schedule may be a TimeInterval or IterationInterval.\n\nA run never silently replaces state files already in path. A simulation restored with restore! continues the numbering after the files up to its restart iteration. Any other existing files raise an error unless overwrite=true, which removes them: all state files for a run that starts at iteration 0, and the files after the restart iteration for a restored run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.NetCDFOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.EnergyDiagnosticsOutput","page":"Core Types","text":"EnergyDiagnosticsOutput(; path=\"output/diagnostic\", schedule, overwrite=false)\n\nConfiguration for simulation-owned energy time-series files. path is the diagnostic directory itself, and schedule may be time- or iteration-based. The files are rewritten as the run goes, at most every few seconds of wall time, and once more when it ends.\n\nA restored simulation appends to the series already in path. Any other existing series raises an error unless overwrite=true, which replaces it (for a restored run, only the records after the restart time).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.EnergyDiagnosticsOutput"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.restore!","page":"Core Types","text":"restore!(model, path)\nrestore!(simulation, path)\n\nRestore the prognostic q and B fields from a simulation state file and rebuild the model's diagnostic fields. The file must be for a domain of the same size and resolution; a different f or N² warns.\n\nRestoring a Simulation also sets its clock to the file's time and iteration, so output, diagnostics, and particles continue from there, and stop_time and stop_iteration keep counting from the original start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.restore!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#QGYBJplus.AnalyticalProfile","page":"Core Types","text":"AnalyticalProfile{T} <: StratificationProfile{T}\n\nAnalytical stratification profile from a user-provided function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.AnalyticalProfile"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#performance","page":"Performance Tips","text":"","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#Choose-an-architecture","page":"Performance Tips","text":"A GPU is the fastest way to run large grids: build the grid with RectilinearGrid(GPU(); ...) after using CUDA. On a CPU, start Julia with several threads (julia --threads=auto) so that the KernelAbstractions kernels run in parallel, and call FFTW.set_num_threads before building the model to thread the transforms too. See CPU and GPU execution.\n\nEvery model array lives in the GPU's memory. A running model holds about 16 complex (nz, nx, ny) arrays' worth of storage, roughly 260 bytes per grid point, or about 2.2 GB for 256×256×128:\n\nStorage Arrays\nFields: q, B, ψ, A, A_z, and the real u, v, w 6.5\nETD-RK2 workspace: stage q and B, and their first-stage tendencies 4\nScratch pool for transforms and products (high-water mark) 5\nTridiagonal solver scratch (real) 0.5\n\nOutput, energy diagnostics, and particle velocities reuse the scratch pool. The exceptions are cuFFT, whose plans need work space of their own, and energy diagnostics with the original YBJ() formulation, which keep one copy of the fields (6.5 arrays). Check that this fits the device before choosing a resolution.","title":"Choose an architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#Single-precision","page":"Performance Tips","text":"The grid's floating-point type sets the precision of every model array:\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(GPU(), Float32; size=(512, 512, 128), extent=(500e3, 500e3, 4000.0))\n\nFloat32 halves the memory above, to about 130 bytes per grid point, and runs much faster on GPUs with reduced double-precision throughput, which includes most consumer cards. Single precision carries about seven significant digits. In the test suite a coupled run tracks the Float64 run to about 1e-4 relative error over a few steps, and round-off grows over long integrations, so keep Float64 for conservation checks and comparisons of small differences. Physical parameters are given as usual and converted, and output files and energy series are written in Float64 either way.","title":"Single precision"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#Reuse-the-simulation-timestepper","page":"Performance Tips","text":"Simulation reuses one ExponentialRungeKutta2Workspace after the first step. For manual field-only stepping, reuse the timestepper object:\n\ntimestepper = ExponentialRungeKutta2(Δt=10.0)\nfor _ in 1:100\n    step!(model, timestepper)\nend\n\nConstructing a new timestepper inside the loop defeats workspace reuse. Manual stepping does not advance particles, output, diagnostics, or a clock.","title":"Reuse the simulation timestepper"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#Avoid-host-transfers-in-the-time-loop","page":"Performance Tips","text":"Avoid copying full fields to the host (Array(model.fields.q)) during the time loop.\nWrite only the fields required for analysis.\nMeasure with output disabled before diagnosing solver performance.\nParticles are advanced on the model's architecture, in one kernel per step over all particles, so they add no host transfer to the time loop; only particle output and the in-memory history copy positions to the host. The tracker keeps the horizontal velocity at both ends of the particle step: four grid-sized real arrays in the particles' precision on that architecture, or eight with wave_motion = :instantaneous.\nCall finalize_simulation! when a simulation is done. It drops the timestepper workspace, scratch buffers, and any diagnostics snapshot, so the garbage collector can return their device memory.","title":"Avoid host transfers in the time loop"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#Limit-output","page":"Performance Tips","text":"Writing physical ψ, waves, and velocities requires inverse transforms and a copy to the host. Select only the fields required for analysis:\n\nNetCDFOutput(path=\"output\",\n             schedule=IterationInterval(100),\n             fields=(:waves,),\n             velocities=false)","title":"Limit output"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/performance/#Benchmark-reproducibly","page":"Performance Tips","text":"Warm up compilation before measuring: the first step compiles every kernel. On a GPU, synchronize before reading the clock (CUDA.@sync) so that queued kernels are included. Report resolution, architecture (CPU threads or GPU model), Julia version, and output settings with every result.","title":"Benchmark reproducibly"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/architectures/#architectures","page":"CPU and GPU Execution","text":"Every array operation in QGYBJ+.jl is a KernelAbstractions.jl kernel, so the same model code runs on the CPU and on a GPU. The architecture is chosen once, when the grid is built, in the same way as in Oceananigans.jl:\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(CPU(); size=(128, 128, 64), extent=(500e3, 500e3, 4000.0))\n\nCPU() is the default, so RectilinearGrid(size=..., extent=...) is a CPU grid. A model built on a grid allocates its fields, transform plans, scratch buffers, coefficient profiles, and dealiasing mask on the grid's architecture.","title":"CPU and GPU execution"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/architectures/#Running-on-a-GPU","page":"CPU and GPU Execution","text":"Load CUDA.jl and pass GPU() to the grid:\n\nusing CUDA\nusing QGYBJplus\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(GPU(); size=(256, 256, 64), extent=(500e3, 500e3, 4000.0))\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, coriolis=FPlane(f=1e-4),\n                   stratification=ConstantStratification(N²=1e-5))\n\nNothing else in a script changes: set!, Simulation, run!, NetCDF output, energy diagnostics, restore!, and particles work as they do on the CPU. Model arrays are CuArrays, the kernels run on CUDA.CUDABackend(), and the horizontal transforms use cuFFT.\n\nGPU() uses the current CUDA device. Choose another device before building the grid with CUDA.device!(n). One model runs on one GPU; multi-GPU decomposition is not supported. To fit a larger grid, or to run faster on a card with reduced double-precision throughput, build the grid in single precision, RectilinearGrid(GPU(), Float32; ...) (see Performance).\n\nAny other KernelAbstractions backend can be passed explicitly as GPU(backend). Its arrays must support AbstractFFTs plans over dimensions (2, 3); otherwise add a method of QGYBJplus.Transforms.horizontal_fft_plans for that array type, as in Testing without a GPU.\n\nKernels run on the backend that their arrays report (KernelAbstractions.get_backend). For CUDA that is CUDABackend() with default options, so launch options such as GPU(CUDABackend(; prefer_blocks=true)) are not used, and a warning says so.\n\nKernelAbstractions also exports names CPU and GPU. A script that loads both packages with using must qualify them, as in QGYBJplus.GPU().","title":"Running on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/architectures/#Multithreaded-CPU-runs","page":"CPU and GPU Execution","text":"On the CPU, kernels run on KernelAbstractions' CPU backend and use Julia's threads. Start Julia with several threads to use them:\n\njulia --threads=8 --project=. examples/asselin_jpo2020.jl\n\nFFTW keeps its own thread count, which defaults to one. Plans are made when the model is built, so set it first to thread the transforms as well:\n\nusing FFTW\nFFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads())\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid)","title":"Multithreaded CPU runs"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/architectures/#What-runs-where","page":"CPU and GPU Execution","text":"Work Location\nTime stepping, elliptic solves, transforms, velocities, energy sums architecture of the grid\nInitial-condition functions passed to set!, FieldFile reads host, then copied to the device\nNetCDF output and restart files device data copied to the host when written\nParticle positions, the velocity that advects them, and the advection kernel architecture of the grid; positions are copied to the host when output or the in-memory history records them\nGrid coordinates (grid.x, grid.kx, …) host\n\nUser functions passed to set!, such as ψ=(x, y, z) -> ..., are evaluated on the host, so they need not be GPU-compatible.","title":"What runs where"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/architectures/#Working-with-model-arrays","page":"CPU and GPU Execution","text":"Copy device data to the host with Array before indexing it element by element, and use on_architecture to move arrays in either direction:\n\nq_host = Array(model.fields.q)                     # host copy of a device field\nmask = on_architecture(architecture(model), dealias_mask(model.grid))\n\nWhole-array operations such as maximum(abs, model.fields.B), sum, fill!, and copyto! work directly on device arrays. Indexing a single element of a device array from the host is disallowed by CUDA.jl outside interactive use.","title":"Working with model arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/architectures/#testing-without-gpu","page":"CPU and GPU Execution","text":"The test suite runs the device code path on the CPU with JLArrays, the reference GPU array implementation, which rejects scalar indexing. JLArrays has no FFT library, so the tests supply plans that round-trip through FFTW:\n\nusing JLArrays, FFTW, QGYBJplus\n\nstruct HostRoundTripPlan{P, B}\n    plan::P\n    buffer::B\nend\n\nfunction Base.:*(plan::HostRoundTripPlan, x::JLArray)\n    copyto!(plan.buffer, x)\n    plan.plan * plan.buffer\n    return copyto!(x, plan.buffer)\nend\n\nfunction QGYBJplus.Transforms.horizontal_fft_plans(buffer::JLArray{<:Complex, 3})\n    host = Array(buffer)\n    return (HostRoundTripPlan(FFTW.plan_fft!(host, (2, 3)), host),\n            HostRoundTripPlan(FFTW.plan_ifft!(host, (2, 3)), host))\nend\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(GPU(JLBackend()); size=(16, 16, 8), extent=(2π, 2π, 1.0))\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid)\n\ntest/test_architectures.jl checks that such a model reproduces the CPU model for every wave formulation, including output, diagnostics, restart, and particles, and that no kernel is handed a host array. It cannot exercise cuFFT or a GPU math library, so run a short CUDA simulation before relying on a new GPU setup.","title":"Testing without a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Troubleshooting","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Construction-fails","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"All resolution and extent values must be positive.\nFPlane(f=...) requires a finite, nonzero Coriolis frequency.\nStratification values must be finite and positive.\n\nStart with a small model and verbose=true to see the runtime summary.","title":"Construction fails"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#GPU()-throws-an-ArgumentError","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"GPU() needs a GPU package. Load CUDA.jl before building the grid:\n\nusing CUDA\nusing QGYBJplus\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(GPU(); size=(64, 64, 32), extent=(1e5, 1e5, 4e3))\n\nIf CUDA.functional() is false, CUDA.jl cannot reach a driver or device; see the CUDA.jl documentation.","title":"GPU() throws an ArgumentError"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#\"Scalar-indexing-is-disallowed\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Model arrays on a GPU cannot be indexed element by element from the host. Copy them first, or use whole-array operations:\n\nq = Array(model.fields.q)     # host copy\nq[1, 2, 3]\nmaximum(abs, model.fields.q)  # runs on the device","title":"\"Scalar indexing is disallowed\""},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Output-is-missing-a-field","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Choose fields in NetCDFOutput:\n\nNetCDFOutput(path=\"output\",\n             fields=(:ψ, :waves),\n             velocities=true)\n\nThe prognostic spectral arrays and vertical coefficients are always written.","title":"Output is missing a field"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#A-run-cannot-be-restarted","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"restore! requires a compatible model: the same resolution, domain extent, wave formulation, and feedback convention. It restores q and B; restoring a Simulation also sets its clock to the file's time and iteration. State files written on a CPU restart on a GPU and vice versa.","title":"A run cannot be restarted"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Output-files-already-exist","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"A run never silently replaces earlier output. A restored simulation continues the files in its output directory; any other existing state, energy, or particle files raise an error. Choose a new directory, or pass overwrite=true to NetCDFOutput, EnergyDiagnosticsOutput, or ParticleOutputManager to replace them.","title":"Output files already exist"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#A-run-is-slow","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Let one Simulation own the complete run instead of constructing a timestepper for every step.\nReduce output frequency and omit unused physical fields.\nAvoid copying fields to the host inside the time loop.\nOn a CPU, start Julia with several threads; see Performance.","title":"A run is slow"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Report-a-reproducible-issue","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Include Julia and package versions, the architecture (CPU threads or GPU model and CUDA version), global resolution, the focused component choices, Δt, and the smallest script that reproduces the problem.","title":"Report a reproducible issue"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/simulation/#simulation-guide","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Simulation owns execution concerns separately from QGYBJModel.\n\nsimulation = Simulation(\n    model;\n    Δt=10.0,\n    stop_time=86400.0,\n    output=false,\n    diagnostics=IterationInterval(100),\n    verbose=true,\n)\n\nSpecify either stop_time or stop_iteration. ETD-RK2 is created automatically and stored in simulation.timestepper.\n\nThe diagnostics schedule records energy time series under the state-output directory's diagnostic/ subdirectory. Use EnergyDiagnosticsOutput(path=..., schedule=...) to choose that directory explicitly, or diagnostics=false to disable diagnostic files.","title":"Running simulations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/simulation/#Lifecycle","page":"Running Simulations","text":"The lifecycle progresses through Ready, Running, and Stopped. An exception moves it to Failed; explicit cleanup moves it to Finalized. Output is set up before the run starts, so an error there, such as files left by another run, leaves the simulation Ready to run again with other settings. Each Simulation is a one-shot execution owner: after it reaches Stopped, construct a new simulation around the same model to continue from the current fields. A new simulation's clock starts at zero; to continue the time axis and the output files of the previous run, restore its last state file into the new simulation with restore!(simulation, path), which also sets the clock (see I/O and restart). After every step the simulation checks for non-finite values and for a blowing-up streamfunction, and enters Failed if either occurs.\n\ntry\n    run!(simulation)\nfinally\n    finalize_simulation!(simulation)\nend\n\nRepeated finalization is safe. A finalized simulation cannot be run again.","title":"Lifecycle"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/simulation/#Overrides-at-run-time","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Before the initial run, run! accepts scoped overrides such as Δt, stop_iteration, output, and diagnostics. Constructor configuration is preferred for reproducible runs:\n\nrun!(simulation; stop_iteration=20, verbose=false)\n\nChanging Δt does not alter an existing stop_time; the simulation advances until the clock first reaches or crosses that time. Output-path, interval, and field-selection overrides rebuild the effective NetCDFOutput configuration before its manager opens.\n\nSet progress=true to print the maximum wave and flow speeds at the diagnostics_interval cadence:\n\nrun!(simulation; progress=true, diagnostics_interval=100)\n\nIteration: 0100, time: 3.333 minutes, Δt: 2 seconds, max(|LA|) = 9.998e-02 m s⁻¹, max(|uₕ|) = 3.349e-01 m s⁻¹, wall time: 12.345 seconds\n\nHere the wave speed is max LA and the flow speed is max sqrtu^2+v^2. Wall time is the elapsed time for the current run! call.\n\nBy default, model construction also reports the architecture and resolution:\n\n┌ Info: QGYBJModel runtime initialized\n│   architecture = GPU(CUDABackend)\n└   size = (256, 256, 128)","title":"Overrides at run time"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/simulation/#Clock-access","page":"Running Simulations","text":"simulation.clock.time\nsimulation.clock.iteration\nget_time(simulation, 10)\ninertial_period(simulation)","title":"Clock access"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/simulation/#Manual-model-stepping","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Low-level applications may construct ExponentialRungeKutta2 and call step!(model, timestepper). That call advances model fields only; it does not update a clock, particles, output, or diagnostics. Ordinary runs should use Simulation.","title":"Manual model stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/interpolation/#interpolation","page":"Interpolation","text":"Particle interpolation evaluates the tracker's copy of the cell-centred horizontal velocity at particle positions, in the advection kernel on the model's architecture. Every scheme reproduces the grid values exactly.","title":"Particle interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/interpolation/#Select-a-method","page":"Interpolation","text":"Value Stencil Error for smooth fields Interpolant\nTRILINEAR 2 points per direction O(h²) continuous\nTRICUBIC (default) 4 points per direction, Catmull-Rom O(h³) continuously differentiable\nQUINTIC 6 points per direction, degree-5 Lagrange O(h⁶) continuous\n\nA three-dimensional evaluation reads 8 grid points per field for TRILINEAR, 64 for TRICUBIC, and 216 for QUINTIC. ADAPTIVE is deprecated and now uses TRICUBIC.\n\nconfiguration = ParticleConfig{Float64}(\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces),\n    x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces),\n    y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    z_level=-100.0,\n    interpolation_method=QUINTIC,\n)\ninitialize_particles!(model, configuration)","title":"Select a method"},{"category":"section","location":"advanced/interpolation/#Boundaries","page":"Interpolation","text":"Horizontally the stencils wrap periodically. Vertically, each column is extended past the lid and the bottom by its mirror image, evenly for the horizontal velocity, which has zero vertical derivative there (psi_z = 0), so the interpolation keeps its order of accuracy up to the boundaries. Positions outside the column take the boundary value. The lower-level interpolate_velocity_advanced also interpolates a vertical velocity, mirrored oddly so that it vanishes at both boundaries; it reads host arrays.","title":"Boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"api/grid_state/#api-grid-state","page":"Geometry & Fields","text":"","title":"Geometry and fields"},{"category":"section","location":"api/grid_state/#Global-geometry","page":"Geometry & Fields","text":"grid = RectilinearGrid(size=(64, 48, 24),\n                       x=(-π, π), y=(-2π, 2π), z=(-1.0, 0.0))\nkx, ky = grid.kx, grid.ky\narchitecture(grid)   # CPU()\n\nUseful properties are size, extent, origin, z_bounds, x, y, z, x_faces, y_faces, z_faces, scalar spacings, kx, ky, and kh2. The wavenumbers follow FFTW ordering and can be used directly with fft. These coordinate vectors always live on the host, whatever the architecture.","title":"Global geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"api/grid_state/#Model-owned-arrays","page":"Geometry & Fields","text":"model.fields is a ModelFields value. Arrays use (z, x, y) order:\n\nField Space Role\nq complex spectral prognostic generalized potential vorticity\nB complex spectral prognostic wave envelope\npsi complex spectral diagnosed streamfunction\nA, C complex spectral diagnosed wave quantities\nu, v, w real physical diagnosed velocities\n\nfields_copy = copy_fields(model.fields)\n\ncopy_fields copies every array and keeps the copies on the fields' architecture. Time steps update the arrays in place, so both a cached fields = model.fields and a cached array such as q = model.fields.q remain live handles. Use copy_fields when a fixed snapshot is needed.","title":"Model-owned arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"api/grid_state/#Arrays-on-a-GPU","page":"Geometry & Fields","text":"On a GPU, model arrays are device arrays such as CuArray. Copy one to the host with Array before indexing elements, and move host data onto the model's architecture with on_architecture:\n\nq_host = Array(model.fields.q)\ncopyto!(model.fields.q, on_architecture(architecture(model), q_host))","title":"Arrays on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#numerical-methods","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"","title":"Numerical methods"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#Horizontal-discretization","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"Horizontal derivatives are pseudo-spectral. Linear derivatives multiply Fourier coefficients by im*kx or im*ky; nonlinear products are evaluated in physical space and transformed back.\n\nThe two-thirds mask retains the integer mode indices inside an ellipse,\n\nbeginaligned\nfraci_x^2K_x^2 + fraci_y^2K_y^2  1  3 mid n_x text or  3 mid n_y \nfraci_x^2K_x^2 + fraci_y^2K_y^2 leq 1  textotherwise\nendaligned\nqquad\nK_x = leftlfloorfracn_x3rightrfloorquad\nK_y = leftlfloorfracn_y3rightrfloor\n\nwhich is a disk on a square grid. Every retained mode then has i_x  n_x3 and i_y  n_y3, so a quadratic product of retained modes never aliases onto a retained mode. The strict inequality excludes the exact k=n3 boundary when a dimension is divisible by three, where two cutoff modes could otherwise alias onto the opposite retained boundary. A dimension with fewer than three points keeps only its mean.\n\nmask = dealias_mask(model.grid)\nis_dealiased(2, 1, model.grid)","title":"Horizontal discretization"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#Vertical-discretization","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"Vertical nodes are uniform cell centers. Second-order differences discretize vertical derivatives and the variable-coefficient operator\n\npartial_zleft(a(z)partial_z phiright)\nqquad a(z)=f^2N^2(z)\n\nAt every horizontal Fourier mode, elliptic inversions reduce to a tridiagonal vertical solve. The pointwise N² profile is sampled at cell centers, while the conservative flux uses a_ell[k] = f² / N²(grid.z_faces[k + 1]) at each cell's upper face. Every vertical column is solved by one kernel work item with the Thomas algorithm, so all horizontal modes are solved in parallel on the CPU or GPU.","title":"Vertical discretization"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#ETD-RK2","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"For\n\nu_t = Lu + N(u)\n\nthe two stages are\n\nbeginaligned\na = e^hLu_n + hvarphi_1(hL)N(u_n)\nu_n+1 = e^hLu_n + hvarphi_1(hL)N(u_n)\n          + hvarphi_2(hL)leftN(a)-N(u_n)right\nendaligned\n\nThe diagonal linear term is horizontal hyperdiffusion,\n\nlambda = nu_1(k_x^2+k_y^2)^p_12\n          + nu_2(k_x^2+k_y^2)^p_22\n\nHere each p_i is the configured total (positive, even) derivative order.\n\nThe implementation evaluates exp(-λh), hφ₁(-λh), and hφ₂(-λh) with cancellation-safe series near zero. Advection, refraction, dispersion, and vertical diffusion are evaluated at both stages.\n\nsimulation = Simulation(model; Δt=10.0, stop_iteration=100)\nrun!(simulation)\n\nFor manual control:\n\ntimestepper = ExponentialRungeKutta2(Δt=10.0)\nstep!(model, timestepper)\n\nManual step! calls do not update a simulation clock, particles, output, or diagnostic schedules. Use run! for ordinary simulations.","title":"ETD-RK2"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#Stability","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"The horizontal dissipative term is integrated exactly and does not impose an explicit diffusion limit. Choose Δt for advective CFL, explicit dispersion, and vertical-diffusion constraints.\n\nDispersion is explicit, and a two-stage Runge–Kutta step amplifies an oscillation of frequency omega by sqrt1+(omegaDelta t)^44 per step. Under YBJ⁺ the fastest envelope frequency is 2f, carried by the depth-mean part of B (LA has no depth mean, so B=nabla^2A4 there), so keep 2fDelta t small: at 2fDelta t = 01 the amplification is 1+13times10^-5 per step, while at 2fDelta t gtrsim 05 round-off in that mode grows visibly over long runs. The original YBJ dispersion is unbounded at high horizontal wavenumber and weak stratification, which is the stiffness YBJ⁺ removes.\n\nDimensional helper functions can target an e-folding time at a selected horizontal scale:\n\nhyperdiffusion = compute_hyperdiff_params(\n    nx=128,\n    ny=128,\n    Lx=70e3,\n    Ly=70e3,\n    dt=10.0,\n    order=4,\n    efold_steps=10,\n)","title":"Stability"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#Transforms-and-kernels","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"ModelRuntime owns the transform plans, reusable work arrays, coefficient profiles, and the dealiasing mask, all on the model's architecture. Horizontal transforms are one batched 2D FFT over every vertical level (FFTW on the CPU, cuFFT on CUDA GPUs), and every other array loop is a KernelAbstractions kernel. Application operators accept the model:\n\ninvert_q_to_psi!(model)\ninvert_B_to_A!(model)\ncompute_velocities!(model)\n\nSee CPU and GPU execution.","title":"Transforms and kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#Array-layout","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"All model arrays use (z, x, y) ordering. Spectral fields are complex and physical velocities are real. NetCDF output converts to (x, y, z).","title":"Array layout"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/numerical_methods/#Verification","page":"Numerical Methods","text":"The test suite checks ETD integrating-factor damping, workspace reuse, second-order convergence, conservation properties, and that the GPU code path (run on the CPU with JLArrays) reproduces the CPU model.","title":"Verification"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stratification/#stratification","page":"Stratification","text":"The vertical coordinate spans negative depth to zero and is cell centered. Runtime coefficients contain N² evaluated at the cell centers model.grid.z, together with N²_face evaluated at each cell's upper face model.grid.z_faces[2:end]. The Boussinesq elliptic coefficient is the face-based array a_ell = f²/N²_face.","title":"Stratification"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stratification/#Constant-profile","page":"Stratification","text":"stratification = ConstantStratification(N²=1e-5)\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, stratification=stratification)","title":"Constant profile"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stratification/#Analytical-profile","page":"Stratification","text":"N²_function = z -> 1e-5 + 4e-5 * exp(-((z + 100.0) / 40.0)^2)\nprofile = AnalyticalProfile(N²_function; returns=:N²)\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, stratification=profile)\n\nUse returns=:N when the function returns buoyancy frequency instead of its square. precision=Float32 selects a different profile precision.","title":"Analytical profile"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stratification/#Built-in-profiles","page":"Stratification","text":"The exported profile families include ConstantN, SkewedGaussian, TanhProfile, ExponentialProfile, PiecewiseProfile, and FileProfile. They can be evaluated independently:\n\nvalues = compute_stratification_profile(profile, grid)\nvalue_at_depth = evaluate_N2(profile, -100.0)\n\ncompute_stratification_profile returns the cell-center samples used by pointwise physics.","title":"Built-in profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stratification/#File-input","page":"Stratification","text":"load_stratification_from_file reads a tabulated profile from a NetCDF file, or from a JLD2 file when the name ends in .jld2:\n\nprofile = load_stratification_from_file(\"stratification.nc\")\nprofile = load_stratification_from_file(\"ctd.jld2\"; group=\"cast12\")\nmodel = QGYBJModel(grid=grid, stratification=profile)\n\nThe file holds a vertical coordinate named z or depth and N² named N2, N², or n2, as real arrays of equal length. depth is positive downward and z positive upward; a z without negative values is read as depth. group selects a NetCDF group, or a JLD2 group, NamedTuple, or Dict; JLD2 arrays may also be ranges. Data in any other form can be passed as FileProfile{Float64}(name, depths, N²), with positive depths in increasing order.\n\nThe data need not be sorted or lie on the model's levels. The model interpolates the profile linearly in depth to its own cell centers and upper faces, so one file serves any vertical resolution. Each level takes the value at its depth rather than an average over the cell, so smooth or bin-average a finely sampled profile to the model's resolution first. Beyond the data, N² is held at the nearest data value, and the model warns when this leaves more than one grid spacing uncovered at the top or bottom of the domain.\n\nInspect external values before model construction:\n\nerrors, warnings = validate_stratification(values)\nisempty(errors) || error(join(errors, \"\\n\"))\n\nModel construction requires finite, positive values. The validation helper also reports unusually weak or strong stratification.","title":"File input"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#getting_started","page":"Installation","text":"","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Install-the-package","page":"Installation","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/subhk/QGYBJplus.jl\")\n\nVerify that Julia can load it:\n\njulia -e 'using QGYBJplus'","title":"Install the package"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Work-from-a-checkout","page":"Installation","text":"git clone https://github.com/subhk/QGYBJplus.jl\ncd QGYBJplus.jl\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); Pkg.test()'","title":"Work from a checkout"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Run-on-several-CPU-threads","page":"Installation","text":"Kernels use Julia's threads on the CPU:\n\njulia --threads=auto --project=. examples/asselin_jpo2020.jl","title":"Run on several CPU threads"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Run-on-a-GPU","page":"Installation","text":"Add CUDA.jl to the environment and pass GPU() to the grid:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"CUDA\")\n\nusing CUDA, QGYBJplus\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(GPU(); size=(128, 128, 64), extent=(500e3, 500e3, 4000.0))\n\nThe rest of a script is unchanged. See CPU and GPU execution.\n\nNext, follow the quick start.","title":"Run on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#quickstart","page":"Quick Start","text":"This example evolves waves in an evolving balanced flow and writes scheduled snapshots and energy diagnostics.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#1.-Create-the-model","page":"Quick Start","text":"using QGYBJplus\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(\n    size=(32, 32, 16),\n    x=(-250e3, 250e3),\n    y=(-250e3, 250e3),\n    z=(-4000.0, 0.0),\n)\n\nmodel = QGYBJModel(\n    grid=grid,\n    coriolis=FPlane(f=1e-4),\n    stratification=ConstantStratification(N²=1e-5),\n    inviscid=Inviscid(),\n    flow=EvolvingFlow(),\n    feedback=NoWaveFeedback(),\n    formulation=YBJPlus(),\n)\n\nThe model owns its fields and computational runtime. Global geometry remains available as model.grid.","title":"1. Create the model"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#2.-Set-initial-conditions","page":"Quick Start","text":"set!(\n    model;\n    ψ=(x, y, z) -> 1e3 * sin(2π * x / 500e3) * cos(2π * y / 500e3),\n    pv_method=:barotropic,\n    waves=SurfaceWave(amplitude=0.1, scale=30.0),\n)","title":"2. Set initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#3.-Run-and-clean-up","page":"Quick Start","text":"simulation = Simulation(\n    model;\n    Δt=20.0,\n    stop_iteration=10,\n    output=NetCDFOutput(\n        path=\"output\",\n        schedule=IterationInterval(5),\n    ),\n    diagnostics=IterationInterval(5),\n)\n\ntry\n    run!(simulation)\nfinally\n    finalize_simulation!(simulation)\nend\n\nSnapshots are written under output/; energy time series are written under output/diagnostic/. See I/O and restart for the file schema and configuration for other physical choices.","title":"3. Run and clean up"},{"category":"section","location":"api/particles/#api-particles","page":"Particles","text":"Particles are optional model-owned data. Initialize them after model construction:\n\nconfiguration = ParticleConfig{Float64}(\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces),\n    x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces),\n    y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    z_level=-100.0,\n    nx_particles=16,\n    ny_particles=16,\n    interpolation_method=TRILINEAR,\n)\ninitialize_particles!(model, configuration)\n\nrun!(simulation) advances installed particles after each model step. A manual update takes the model's current state as the velocity at the end of the particle step, so advance the model over the same interval first:\n\nstep!(model, timestepper)\nadvect_particles!(model, timestepper.Δt; current_time=t)","title":"Particle API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/particles/#Distribution-helpers","page":"Particles","text":"","title":"Distribution helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/particles/#Interpolation","page":"Particles","text":"Available methods are TRILINEAR, TRICUBIC (default), and QUINTIC; ADAPTIVE is deprecated and now uses TRICUBIC. See Particle interpolation. Particles move with the horizontal velocity and keep their depth; they are advected on the model's architecture, where the tracker keeps its copy of the velocity.\n\nUse ParticleConfig3D or the distribution helpers to release particles at several depths, in volumes, layers, random points, or explicit positions.","title":"Interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/particles/#Output","page":"Particles","text":"Particle trajectory helpers remain separate from Eulerian NetCDF snapshots:","title":"Output"},{"category":"type","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.ParticleConfig","page":"Particles","text":"ParticleConfig{T}(; x_min=0, x_max, y_min=0, y_max, z_level, kwargs...)\n\nConfigure a regular horizontal particle grid at z_level. nx_particles and ny_particles default to 10. A positive particle_advec_time delays release. Particles move with the horizontal velocity and keep their depth.\n\nintegration_method selects the trajectory integrator: :euler, :rk2 (default), or :rk4. Each integration stage interpolates the velocity linearly in time between the start and the end of the particle step, so in an evolving flow :rk2 and :rk4 are second-order accurate in time and :euler first-order. :rk4 is still more accurate per step where trajectories curve strongly.\n\ninterpolation_method selects the spatial interpolation: TRILINEAR (O(h²)), TRICUBIC (default; Catmull-Rom, O(h³)), or QUINTIC (degree-5 Lagrange, O(h⁶)). ADAPTIVE is deprecated and now uses TRICUBIC.\n\nA finite save_interval makes the tracker keep an in-memory history of the positions every save_interval (every step for 0), up to max_save_points records, for write_particle_trajectories. The default, Inf, keeps none: simulations write trajectories with a ParticleOutputManager.\n\nwave_motion selects how particles feel the near-inertial waves:\n\n:averaged (default): phase-averaged trajectories, advected by the Lagrangian-mean velocity of the balanced flow, ∇⊥ψ. As ψ is the Lagrangian-mean streamfunction (Xie & Vanneste 2015; Wagner & Young 2016), this already contains the waves' Stokes drift.\n:instantaneous: trajectories that follow the inertial oscillation, advected by the wave velocity e^{-ift} LA, with the phase evaluated at each integration stage, plus the Lagrangian mean minus the Stokes drift of horizontal orbits. The orbits make up that part of the drift themselves, so over an inertial period the particles drift with the Lagrangian mean, as with :averaged; the part of the Stokes drift from vertical excursions, which particles that keep their depth do not make, stays in the mean. The particle time step must resolve the inertial period (f Δt ≲ 0.2).\n\nuse_ybj_w and use_3d_advection were removed and must stay false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.ParticleConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.ParticleConfig3D","page":"Particles","text":"ParticleConfig3D{T}(; x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max,\n                    z_min, z_max, kwargs...)\n\nConfigure a volume, layered distribution, random cloud, or explicit particle positions using explicit coordinate bounds. Prefer particles_in_grid_3d, particles_in_layers, particles_random_3d, or particles_custom, which populate the bounds and distribution-specific fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.ParticleConfig3D"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.particles_in_box","page":"Particles","text":"particles_in_box([T=Float32], z_level;\n                 x_min=0, x_max, y_min=0, y_max, nx=10, ny=10, kwargs...)\n\nCreate an nx by ny particle grid at the coordinate z_level. The default storage type is Float32; pass Float64 as the first argument when needed.\n\nconfig = particles_in_box(\n    Float64,\n    -500.0;\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces), x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces), y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    nx=20, ny=20,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.particles_in_box"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_in_circle","page":"Particles","text":"particles_in_circle(z; center, radius, n, pattern, kwargs...)\n\nCreate particles distributed in a circular disk at a fixed z-level.\n\nArguments\n\nz: Vertical level where particles are placed\ncenter: (x, y) center of circle (default: (π, π))\nradius: Radius of the circular region (default: 1.0)\nn: Number of particles (default: 100)\npattern: Distribution pattern (default: :sunflower)\n:sunflower - Fibonacci spiral (very uniform, recommended)\n:rings - Concentric rings\n:random - Uniform random within disk\n\nExample\n\n# 100 particles in a circle at z = -π/2\nconfig = particles_in_circle(-π/2; radius=1.0, n=100)\n\n# Custom center and larger circle\nconfig = particles_in_circle(-1.0; center=(2.0, 2.0), radius=2.0, n=200)\n\n# Random distribution\nconfig = particles_in_circle(-π/4; radius=0.5, n=50, pattern=:random)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_in_circle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_in_grid_3d","page":"Particles","text":"particles_in_grid_3d(; x_max, y_max, z_max, nx, ny, nz, x_min=0, y_min=0, z_min=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nCreate an nx by ny by nz regular particle grid.\n\nWhen z_min is supplied, z_min and z_max are coordinates. When it is omitted, z_max is interpreted as a positive depth and the vertical interval is [-z_max, 0].\n\nconfig = particles_in_grid_3d(\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces), x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces), y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    z_min=first(model.grid.z_faces), z_max=last(model.grid.z_faces),\n    nx=10, ny=10, nz=10, precision=Float64,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_in_grid_3d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_in_layers","page":"Particles","text":"particles_in_layers(z_levels; x_max, y_max, nx, ny, x_min=0, y_min=0, kwargs...)\n\nCreate an nx by ny particle grid at every coordinate in z_levels.\n\nconfig = particles_in_layers(\n    [-500.0, -1000.0, -1500.0];\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces), x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces), y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    nx=10, ny=10, precision=Float64,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_in_layers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_random_3d","page":"Particles","text":"particles_random_3d(n; x_max, y_max, z_max, x_min=0, y_min=0, z_min=nothing, seed=1234, kwargs...)\n\nCreate n uniformly random particles in a rectangular volume. seed defaults to 1234. The z_min/z_max convention matches particles_in_grid_3d.\n\nconfig = particles_random_3d(\n    500;\n    x_min=first(model.grid.x_faces), x_max=last(model.grid.x_faces),\n    y_min=first(model.grid.y_faces), y_max=last(model.grid.y_faces),\n    z_min=first(model.grid.z_faces), z_max=last(model.grid.z_faces),\n    precision=Float64,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_random_3d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_custom","page":"Particles","text":"particles_custom(positions; kwargs...)\n\nCreate particles at the supplied (x, y, z) coordinates.\n\nconfig = particles_custom([\n    (1.0, 1.0, -50.0),\n    (2.0, 2.0, -100.0),\n    (3.0, 1.5, -150.0),\n]; precision=Float64)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.UnifiedParticleAdvection.EnhancedParticleConfig.particles_custom"},{"category":"type","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.ParticleOutputManager","page":"Particles","text":"ParticleOutputManager(output_dir; save_interval_iter=0,\n                      save_interval_time=0,\n                      output_mode=:trajectory,\n                      file_prefix=\"particles\",\n                      overwrite=false)\n\nSchedule particle NetCDF output under output_dir/particles, every save_interval_iter iterations or every save_interval_time in the model's time units: give one of them; the iteration interval wins when both are positive. output_mode may be:\n\n:trajectory: one file, <prefix>_trajectory.nc, written when the run ends;\n:streaming: one file, <prefix>_stream.nc, extended at every save, so a run that is killed keeps the trajectories saved so far;\n:snapshots: one file per save, <prefix>_<iteration>.nc.\n\nEach record holds the particle positions and their velocities at the recorded time. Existing files of the same mode and prefix raise an error unless overwrite=true, which removes them. Particle positions are not part of the model restart files, so a restored simulation starts new trajectories.\n\nPass the manager as particle_output when constructing a QGYBJplus.Simulation. The simulation writes initial, scheduled, and final particle states and closes the manager during finalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.ParticleOutputManager"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.write_particle_trajectories","page":"Particles","text":"write_particle_trajectories(filename, tracker; metadata=Dict())\n\nWrite the tracker's in-memory trajectory history to a NetCDF file. The tracker keeps that history only when its configuration sets save_interval. During a simulation a ParticleOutputManager writes trajectories instead; its :streaming mode does so without holding them in memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.write_particle_trajectories"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.read_particle_trajectories","page":"Particles","text":"read_particle_trajectories(filename) -> NamedTuple\n\nRead a trajectory file written by write_particle_trajectories. The returned named tuple contains x, y, and z matrices in (particle, time) order, plus time, particle_ids, and file attributes.\n\ntrajectories = read_particle_trajectories(\"particles.nc\")\ninitial_x = trajectories.x[:, 1]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.read_particle_trajectories"},{"category":"function","location":"api/particles/#QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.write_particle_snapshot","page":"Particles","text":"write_particle_snapshot(filename, tracker, time)\n\nWrite current particle positions to NetCDF file (single time snapshot).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.ParticleIO.write_particle_snapshot"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#api-physics","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Public operator entry points accept a model so they can use its fields, geometry, coefficients, transform plans, and architecture consistently.","title":"Physics and operator API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Elliptic-inversions","page":"Physics Functions","text":"invert_q_to_psi!(model)\ninvert_B_to_A!(model)","title":"Elliptic inversions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Velocities","page":"Physics Functions","text":"compute_velocities!(model)\ncompute_vertical_velocity!(model)\ncompute_ybj_vertical_velocity!(model)\ncompute_total_velocities!(model; t)   # instantaneous velocity at time t\ncompute_wave_velocities!(model; t)","title":"Velocities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Diagnostics","page":"Physics Functions","text":"flow_energy = flow_kinetic_energy(model)\nB_energy, A_energy = wave_energy(model)","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Coefficient-helpers","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Lower-level array kernels are implementation details. Model-level methods are the supported boundary for applications.","title":"Coefficient helpers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#QGYBJplus.a_ell_from_N2","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Compute a(z) = f² / N²(z), with N² bounded below by √eps times its largest value. The bound is relative so that it means the same in every unit system: an absolute √eps ≈ 1.5e-8 would clamp realistic abyssal N² in s⁻².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.a_ell_from_N2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#QGYBJplus.dealias_mask","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Return the two-thirds dealiasing mask for global geometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.dealias_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#QGYBJplus.is_dealiased","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Test whether the Fourier mode with indices (i, j) lies inside the two-thirds cutoff: the ellipse (iₓ/Kₓ)² + (i_y/K_y)² ≤ 1 in mode index, with Kₓ = ⌊nₓ/3⌋ and K_y = ⌊n_y/3⌋, which is a disk on a square grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.is_dealiased"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#QGYBJplus.compute_hyperdiff_coeff","page":"Physics Functions","text":"compute_hyperdiff_coeff(; dx, dy, dt, order=4, efold_steps=10, kmax_fraction=2/3)\n\nCoefficient ν of a hyperdiffusion term ν kₕ^order that damps the target wavenumber kmax_fraction π / min(dx, dy) by one e-fold every efold_steps steps of length dt.\n\nThe default, kmax_fraction = 2/3, targets the largest wavenumber the two-thirds dealiasing keeps. kmax_fraction = 1 targets the Nyquist wavenumber instead; nonlinear runs remove it, so the smallest scale they keep is then damped (3/2)^order times more weakly than intended.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.compute_hyperdiff_coeff"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#QGYBJplus.compute_hyperdiff_params","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Compute a coefficient and Laplacian power from domain dimensions (see compute_hyperdiff_coeff for kmax_fraction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.compute_hyperdiff_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#QGYBJplus.dimensional_hyperdiff_params","page":"Physics Functions","text":"Return matching typed closure coefficients for flow and waves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QGYBJplus.dimensional_hyperdiff_params"},{"category":"section","location":"#QGYBJ.jl","page":"Home","text":"QGYBJ+.jl simulates interactions between quasigeostrophic balanced flow and near-inertial waves. It combines horizontally pseudo-spectral operators, second-order vertical differences, and ETD-RK2 time integration, with every array operation written as a KernelAbstractions.jl kernel so that the same model runs on CPUs and GPUs.\n\nStart with installation, then run the quick-start example. The Asselin dipole walkthrough is the complete research-scale example.","title":"QGYBJ+.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Minimal-workflow","page":"Home","text":"using QGYBJplus\n\ngrid = RectilinearGrid(\n    size=(32, 32, 16),\n    extent=(500e3, 500e3, 4000.0),\n    centered=true,\n)\nmodel = QGYBJModel(\n    grid=grid,\n    coriolis=FPlane(f=1e-4),\n    stratification=ConstantStratification(N²=1e-5),\n    inviscid=Inviscid(),\n    flow=EvolvingFlow(),\n    feedback=NoWaveFeedback(),\n    formulation=YBJPlus(),\n)\nset!(\n    model;\n    ψ=(x, y, z) -> 1e3 * sin(2π * x / 500e3) * cos(2π * y / 500e3),\n    waves=SurfaceWave(amplitude=0.1, scale=30.0),\n)\n\nsimulation = Simulation(\n    model;\n    Δt=20.0,\n    stop_iteration=10,\n    output=NetCDFOutput(\n        path=\"output\",\n        schedule=IterationInterval(5),\n    ),\n)\ntry\n    run!(simulation)\nfinally\n    finalize_simulation!(simulation)\nend\n\nTo run the same script on a GPU, load CUDA.jl and build the grid with RectilinearGrid(GPU(); ...). ETD-RK2 is the sole production stepping scheme.","title":"Minimal workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-owns-what","page":"Home","text":"RectilinearGrid stores immutable global geometry and the architecture (CPU() or GPU()).\nQGYBJModel owns physics, numerics, fields, runtime resources, and optional particles.\nSimulation owns the clock, timestepper, stopping rules, output, diagnostics, and lifecycle.","title":"What owns what"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation-map","page":"Home","text":"Installation\nQuick start\nKey concepts\nAsselin dipole walkthrough\nConfiguration\nPhysics overview\nCPU and GPU execution\nParticle advection\nCore API","title":"Documentation map"},{"category":"section","location":"worked_example/#worked_example","page":"Worked Example","text":"examples/asselin_jpo2020.jl implements the barotropic dipole setup from Asselin et al. (2020) with the composition-first API.","title":"Asselin et al. dipole"},{"category":"section","location":"worked_example/#Run","page":"Worked Example","text":"julia --threads=auto --project=. examples/asselin_jpo2020.jl\n\nTo run on a GPU, load CUDA.jl and build the grid with RectilinearGrid(GPU(); ...); see CPU and GPU execution.\n\nDefaults are 256×256×128 points, a 70 km square horizontal domain, 3 km depth, and 10 inertial periods with Δt = 2 s.\n\nFor a smaller run, reduce size and stop_time in the script. The setup uses a fixed barotropic flow, YBJ⁺ waves, no wave feedback, and wave hyperdiffusion.","title":"Run"},{"category":"section","location":"worked_example/#Output","page":"Worked Example","text":"Snapshots are written below output_asselin/. Use examples/compute_energy.jl for spatial kinetic energy, or see I/O and restart for direct FFTW analysis with grid.kx and grid.ky.","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"worked_example/#Reference","page":"Worked Example","text":"Asselin, O., Thomas, L. N., Young, W. R., & Rainville, L. (2020), “Refraction and Straining of Near-Inertial Waves by Barotropic Eddies”, Journal of Physical Oceanography, 50, 3439–3454.","title":"Reference"}]
}
